            <div xml:id="d588">

                <pb facs="0476" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/10335: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-30T18:00:00+08:00">September 30, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 30—10:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>741. Commander-in-Chief reported in his despatch 0024–1610 attacks upon Chinese
                    fishing fleets off southern coast. Further details are reported in Hong Kong’s
                    telegram of September 29, 4 p.m.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d588-f1"/> This action,
                    it seems to me, merits some comment by us in any case and representations [to]
                    that effect made to the Japanese Government as being contrary to ordinary
                    international practice.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
